  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to enhance the known low-discrepancy construction for minimizing Erds constant C by introducing gradient-based optimization with rotationally invariant constraint handling. The previous approach is ubiquitous and often needs refinement. Here, we focus on several key improvements:\n\n1. Novel Gradient Handling: A stricter treatment of non-circular constraint gradients derived from locally quadratized overlap integrals\n2. Advanced Rotation Dynamics: Incorporation of startpoint selection based on original function stationarity detection\n3. Directed Optimization: Targeted gradient directions moving away from boundary states\n4. Dynamically Adaptive Slicing: Controlled segmentation and zeroscaling in high-irregularity regions\n\nThe primary constraints are:\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x \u2208 [0,2]\n- \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nOur implementation addresses the need for a reproducible, convergent optimization framework by precisely managing these constraints within gradient calculations.\n\nMathematical Specification\nVariables:\n- N: Number of grid points (int), 300+ terms\n- h: Vector of length N, values \u2208 [0,1]\n- Rotations: Rot specification index\n- Alpha: Problem sensitivity scaling, term \u2208 R\n\nObjective Function:\nC5 = max\u2096 (integral h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx)\n\nThis is implemented via:\n- Local quadratization of each shift component\n- Finite-difference matrix assembly with spectral accuracy\n- Sampling of rolls with priority for boundary sensitivity\n\nConstraint Handling:\nThe integral constraint is enforced via:\n- Integral diagonal constraint matrix (diagonal=dx)\n- Gradient offset vector (target value)\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - If provided, start from given initial_h_values (N=300)\n   - Extend via powered zero-padding for improved convergence\n   - Zero-center with spectral gradient clipping\n\n2. Constraint Enforcement:\n   - Clip boundary values <0 to 0, >1 to 1\n   - Adjust sum exactly with constraint Jacobian treatment\n\n3. Core Optimization:\n   - Dynamic autointegration of current solution\n   - Condensed covariance rotation\n   - \u0627\u0644\u0637\u064a\u0641ing for targeted sensitivity reduction\n\n4. Search Step:\n   - Global gradient decomposition\n   - Directional linear combination\n   - Rotomatic evaluation of new states\n\nAlgorithmic Parameters:\nUse iterative refinement with fixed topology. Starting conditions: alpha=0.2, N=300, max_iter=2040. Progress tracking uses time budget slices and dimensional discontinuity labeling. Optimization-direction determines primary gradient adjustment.\n\nValidation and Initialization:\nAlways ensure h satisfies:\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h_i * dx = 1\n\nKey computational performance in and out-of-bounds modes is achieved through efficient integration. In bounds, integration uses masking. Out-of-bounds, use local subdivision up to specified recursive depth.\n\nValidation Requirements:\n- Always check finite obj, grad, cons violations\n- Verify conservation of 1-area integral and 0-out-of-bounds\n- Maintain \u221d\u00b2 spectral stability and \u03c9-limit values\n\nBudget and Termination:\nEach iteration plateaus on \u03b4-integral changes. Run at 600 iterations/100 seconds block. If plateau Longer than 150 seconds, terminate and extract. Global stage is over 2040 iterations.\n\nSpecific high-frequency response:\n<insert detailed mathematics here>\n<pseudocode>\n# Pseudocode for core optimization\nwhile iteration < max_iter and time_budget not exceeded:\n    build_current_condensed_covariance(rot_state)\n    evaluate_rot_derivative()\n    apply_gradient_from_restore(deriv, max_iter, ftol=1e-8)\n    monitor_gradient_norm(\u03c9)\n    if \u03c9 < golden_ratio_threshold or obj_improvement>0:\n        accept_rotation_and_result()\n        recompute_boundaries_from_obj()\n    # Special case handling for boundary rot\n    if obj_increase_significant:\n        flip_bits_until_improvement(derivative_weight)",
